-Deep learning subsequently emerged as a data-driven alternative. Gamper and Tashev (2018) used log-energy features from gammatone ERB subbands as input to a six-layer convolutional neural network (CNN) and evaluated its performance in blind reverberation time estimation on the ACE evaluation corpus. Xiong et al. (2018) formulated joint estimation of $T_{60}$ and the early-to-late reverberation ratio as a classification problem using time–frequency features. Deng et al. (2020) introduced a convolutional recurrent network for online estimation from variable-length speech, and Srivastava et al. (2021) extended this framework to multichannel inputs. Götz et al. (2023) used three convolutional recurrent variants to estimate parameters under dynamic acoustic conditions. Saini and Peissig (2023) demonstrated that a MobileViT-style transformer could run in real time on a smartphone. Duangpummet et al. (2022) extended the scope from full-band $T_{60}$ estimation to the joint estimation of $T_{60}$, early decay time, clarity, and the speech transmission index. Despite their architectural differences, most of these models use the same regressor design: a time–frequency feature extraction network is followed by several fully connected layers that map the aggregated features to a scalar $T_{60}$. To improve robustness in noise, previous studies have combined denoising with $T_{60}$ estimation. Zheng et al. (2022) proposed a two-stage noise-aware framework. A speech and noise separation network is first pretrained and then jointly optimised with the $T_{60}$ estimation network in the second stage. The estimation network uses the original noisy magnitude spectrum together with the estimated magnitude spectra of speech and noise. Zhang et al. (2024) proposed DAMTL, a denoising-aided multi-task learning method for joint end-to-end learning of denoising reconstruction and $T_{60}$ regression. The method uses a shared feature extraction network containing complex-valued convolutions and complex-valued LSTMs.
